Rem Capability: file
Rem ==================================================================
Rem recio.shoddy — Shoddy standard library: record-oriented binary i/o
Rem
Rem a layer over the binary builtins that does the offset arithmetic.
Rem You supply the record size in bytes and a reader / writer function:
Rem
Rem     Def rdstudent(f As Number) As Student
Rem         Student(GetStr(f, 12), GetNum(f))          ' 20-byte record
Rem
Rem     Def wrstudent(f As Number, s As Student)
Rem         PutStr(f, Name(s), 12)
Rem         PutNum(f, Score(s))
Rem
Rem     PutRec(f, 2, 20, wrstudent, Student("GRACE", 91))
Rem     GetRec(f, 2, 20, rdstudent)
Rem
Rem Records are numbered from 1. Effectful, like all file words.
Rem
Rem The twelve binary builtins it is a layer over are documented one by
Rem one immediately below: BOpen TryBOpen BClose Seek BPos BSize PutNum
Rem GetNum PutBool GetBool PutStr GetStr.
Rem ==================================================================

Rem ---- the builtins this machine is built on -------------------------
Rem
Rem NOT Defs. The engine dispatches these, and a Def whose name is a
Rem builtin is refused; they are written here because recio.shoddy is
Rem where a reader goes looking for them. The flat reference is section
Rem 10 of docs/spec.html, and the stack effects below are the ones the
Rem compiler's own Effects.cs table declares.
Rem
Rem CLASSIC BASIC RECORD FILES. A handle is a Number, drawn from the
Rem same table of sixteen that sockets use. Byte positions are 1-BASED,
Rem like everything else in this language. Every transfer word advances
Rem the position by exactly the width it moved, which is what makes
Rem record layouts computable: a Name(12) + Score(8) record is 20 bytes,
Rem so record k starts at 1 + (k - 1) * 20 — which is RecPos below, and
Rem the whole of what this machine adds.
Rem
Rem READING PAST END OF FILE ABORTS. So does over-running a PutStr
Rem field. Everything here is effectful — Main's side of the program.

Rem BOpen(path As String) As Number                   ( path -- h )
Rem     Open path read/write and answer its handle, CREATING the file if
Rem     it is absent. Up to 16 handles may be open at once. Aborts if it
Rem     cannot open.
Rem
Rem     THE OPEN-OR-CREATE IS A TRAP WORTH NAMING: asking whether a
Rem     binary file is there by opening it leaves an empty file behind
Rem     when it was not. TryBOpen is the word for that question.

Rem TryBOpen(path As String) As Result                ( path -- result )
Rem     Ok(handle), or Err(why, 0) with why = CANNOT OPEN 'path' (…)
Rem     from the same closed set TryReadFile uses, plus TOO MANY OPEN
Rem     FILES. It differs from BOpen twice over and both differences are
Rem     the point: the failure is REPORTED rather than fatal, and it
Rem     opens an EXISTING FILE ONLY. Since nothing can learn a file's
Rem     size or its leading bytes without opening it, this is the only
Rem     way to pre-flight a binary read at all.

Rem BClose(h As Number)                               ( h -- )
Rem     Close the handle and free its slot.

Rem Seek(h As Number, pos As Number)                  ( h pos -- )
Rem     Move to byte position pos, counting from 1.

Rem BPos(h As Number) As Number                       ( h -- pos )
Rem     Where the handle is now, counting from 1.

Rem BSize(h As Number) As Number                      ( h -- bytes )
Rem     How many bytes the file holds. RecCount below is this divided by
Rem     the record size.

Rem PutNum(h As Number, x As Number)                  ( h x -- )
Rem     Write x as an 8-byte IEEE double and advance 8.

Rem GetNum(h As Number) As Number                     ( h -- x )
Rem     Read an 8-byte double and advance 8.

Rem PutBool(h As Number, b As Boolean)                ( h b -- )
Rem     Write b as one byte and advance 1.

Rem GetBool(h As Number) As Boolean                   ( h -- b )
Rem     Read one byte as a Boolean and advance 1.

Rem PutStr(h As Number, s As String, width As Number) ( h s width -- )
Rem     Write EXACTLY width bytes: s, zero-padded out to the field. An
Rem     over-length string ABORTS rather than being silently truncated,
Rem     which is what keeps a fixed layout honest.

Rem GetStr(h As Number, width As Number) As String    ( h width -- s )
Rem     Read exactly width bytes and strip the zero padding back off.

Rem ---- this machine's own words --------------------------------------

Def RecPos(k As Number, size As Number) As Number
    1 + (k - 1) * size

Def RecSeek(f As Number, k As Number, size As Number)
    Seek(f, RecPos(k, size))

Def RecCount(f As Number, size As Number) As Number
    Floor(BSize(f) / size)

Def GetRec(f As Number, k As Number, size As Number, reader As [ Number -- t ]) As t
    RecSeek(f, k, size)
    Call(reader, f)

Def PutRec(f As Number, k As Number, size As Number, writer As [ Number t -- ], v As t)
    RecSeek(f, k, size)
    Call(writer, f, v)

Def AppendRec(f As Number, size As Number, writer As [ Number t -- ], v As t)
    PutRec(f, RecCount(f, size) + 1, size, writer, v)

Def AllRecs(f As Number, size As Number, reader As [ Number -- t ]) As List Of t
    Map(Range(1, RecCount(f, size)), Fn(k) => GetRec(f, k, size, reader))
